content.text:
Together, the three commits add 26 lines to 5 upstream OrcaSlicer files and delete or move nothing. Each change is a hook that does nothing unless JusPrin code sets it, so stock OrcaSlicer behaves the same as before. These are the only upstream source files changed since the printer conversation panel was merged. The only other files outside the fork's own directories are `.codex/config.toml` and `AGENTS.md`, which are repo config.

## 1. Skip the first-run setup wizard (b361b84773), 4 lines

**`GUI_App.hpp`** adds a public flag:
```cpp
// Presentation policy for automatic setup only; explicit run_wizard calls remain available.
bool            show_config_wizard_on_startup { true };
```
**`GUI_App.cpp`** makes `config_wizard_startup()` return early when the flag is false:
```cpp
    if (!show_config_wizard_on_startup)
        return false;
```
The JusPrin shell sets the flag to false when it installs, because the Home page now handles first-run printer setup. It restores the saved value when it uninstalls or the window is destroyed. Opening the wizard from a menu still works.

## 2. Choose the printer connection provider directly (9f739dccf2), 10 lines

A "printer agent" is Orca's plugin for talking to a printer, such as Bambu's network agent or a print-host agent. Orca normally picks one from the selected printer profile.

**`GUI_App.hpp`** adds a runtime field, plus `#include <utility>`:
```cpp
// An explicit monitor connection may select a provider independently of slicing.
// Empty IDs retain the stock profile-driven selection. Runtime only.
std::pair<std::string, std::string> printer_agent_override;
```
**`GUI_App.cpp`**: in `switch_printer_agent()`, after Orca has worked out the agent from the profile, the override replaces that choice if it is set:
```cpp
    // Resolve an explicit connection through the same switch/refresh/selection path.
    if (!printer_agent_override.first.empty()) {
        effective_agent_id = printer_agent_override.first;
        cloud_agent_id = printer_agent_override.second;
    }
```
The provider you pick for the connection now stays in place when the slicing profile changes. After the override, Orca's normal steps still run: the registration check, creating the agent, and refreshing the device list.

## 3. Canvas toolbar hooks (ee1bb889a2), 12 lines

**`GLCanvas3D.hpp/.cpp`** adds an optional callback that draws extra ImGui content once per frame. ImGui is the immediate-mode UI library the 3D canvas uses for its overlays. The callback runs right after Orca's own overlays, even when those overlays are hidden:
```cpp
std::function<void()> m_overlay_renderer;
void set_overlay_renderer(std::function<void()> renderer) { m_overlay_renderer = std::move(renderer); set_as_dirty(); }
...
    if (m_overlay_renderer)
        m_overlay_renderer();
```
**`GizmoObjectManipulation.hpp`** adds three public wrappers around existing private reset functions. The fork's new value panel calls these instead of copying Orca's move, rotate, and scale reset logic:
```cpp
void reset_position() { reset_position_value(); }
void reset_rotation(bool to_value_when_tool_opened) { reset_rotation_value(to_value_when_tool_opened); }
void reset_scale() { reset_scale_value(); }
```

## Rebase risk
- **Low:** the wizard flag, the canvas hook, and the gizmo wrappers are all small additions.
- **Needs care:** the `switch_printer_agent()` change. The 9f739dccf2 commit message says upstream has already rewritten this function: the effective agent ID is now `const` and cloud agents are resolved differently. So these 6 lines won't re-apply as they are. At the next rebase, the override has to be ported into the new code, applied after the profile choice and before the agent is created.
- 9f739dccf2 is the only one of the three whose commit message records the rebase check that `agent-docs/jusprin/fork-stewardship.md` requires. The other two don't.

